From 7ac91b97d7712dd8acf7d179e44cbf25d66886b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:46:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wisp: a TCP stream's DATA comes as its reader takes it, by
 stream credit (0xE2), when the server offers it

Wisp's CONTINUE paces what the client sends and nothing paced what the
server sends: a server read each remote as fast as its transport toward
the client drained, whether or not the page read the body. A body read
slowly piled up in epoxy (flume::unbounded per stream), and a remote
done early closed its idle keep-alive while the client still held the
rest. The only brake a server had was per connection or per lane, and it
held back every stream behind one slow reader. Natively TCP's receive
window holds back that one socket.

Stream credit (holyWisp's `stream-credit`, ~/.claude/stomp-work/wisp-credit
SPEC.md, draft 1) gives each TCP and Twisp stream a byte window the
client grants as its reader consumes. The client names extension 0xE2 in
its INFO, with its initial window (u32 LE, 256 KiB), only when the
server's INFO named it; otherwise its INFO is as before (UDP only) and
nothing below happens.

With it on, each TCP stream's reader (what hyper or rustls above it took
from MuxStreamRead) grants CREDIT(consumed + window) once it has consumed
half the window since the last grant, a 13-byte packet (type 0xE2, the
stream id, the absolute limit u64 LE) sent by the actor, only while the
stream is open, on the stream's own lane (STomp routes by stream id). The
window grows as TCP's receive autotuning does: when the reader finds
nothing to read and the data stopped exactly at a limit it was given
(the server had used all of it before the next grant came into effect),
the window was what held the stream back, and it doubles, once per such
stall, up to 16 MiB per stream and within 64 MiB of growth across the
connection's streams, given back when a stream's reader goes. A reader
that stalls is just not granted more, and the server stops reading that
remote alone. UDP streams are never counted. DATA past the limit (a
server's bug) is taken.
